From: Peter Strand <peter@zarquon.se>
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] sep: log the m1n1-time GETRAND handshake (touchid step 5.1)

Instrument m1n1's sep_get_random() to answer the decisive step-5.1 question:
does the SEP mailbox answer at m1n1 time on this specific j413, before Linux is
in the picture? sep_get_random() already pokes SEP ROM endpoint 0xff (GETRAND)
to seed KASLR/RNG during kboot, so it is a ready-made liveness probe; today it
just fails silently ("SEP: couldn't get enough random bytes...").

This adds, once per boot:
  - asc_init() result for /arm-io/sep (did the mailbox device attach at all)
  - asc_cpu_running() + asc_can_recv() before the first send (is the SEP ASC
    alive, and is there a stale reply already sitting in the I2A FIFO)
  - the asc_send() result, and the asc_recv_timeout() result + raw msg0

It also notes a real bug hiding in plain sight: SEP_TIMEOUT is 1000 and
asc_recv_timeout() takes MICROseconds, so the current probe waits only 1 ms.
A slept SEPOS (iBoot sets sepfw-booted=1, then the SEP is slept before OS
handoff) may answer slower than that. So on a 1 ms miss we retry once with a
200 ms window and log that separately, to tell "wedged" apart from "slow".

Debug-only; behaviour on the success path is unchanged (a 1 ms answer skips the
retry). No functional change to the seed derived from a working SEP.
diff --git a/src/sep.c b/src/sep.c
index 7a40fef..c489004 100644
--- a/src/sep.c
+++ b/src/sep.c
@@ -16,14 +16,24 @@
 #define SEP_MSG_GETRAND   16
 #define SEP_REPLY_GETRAND 116
 
+/* NB: asc_recv_timeout() takes MICROseconds, so this is a 1 ms window, not 1 s.
+ * (touchid step 5.1: a slept SEPOS may answer slower than 1 ms.) */
 #define SEP_TIMEOUT 1000
+/* Long fallback window for the step-5.1 probe: distinguishes "wedged" from
+ * "just slower than 1 ms". 200 ms. */
+#define SEP_TIMEOUT_DBG 200000
 
 static asc_dev_t *sep_asc = NULL;
 
+/* touchid step 5.1: emit the getrand handshake detail exactly once per boot. */
+static bool sep_dbg_done = false;
+
 int sep_init(void)
 {
-    if (!sep_asc)
+    if (!sep_asc) {
         sep_asc = asc_init("/arm-io/sep");
+        printf("SEP: [dbg] asc_init(/arm-io/sep) = %p\n", sep_asc);
+    }
     if (!sep_asc)
         return -1;
     return 0;
@@ -45,9 +55,32 @@ size_t sep_get_random(void *buffer, size_t len)
         u32 rng;
         size_t copy;
 
-        if (!asc_send(sep_asc, &msg_getrand))
+        bool dbg = !sep_dbg_done;
+        if (dbg)
+            printf("SEP: [dbg] cpu_running=%d can_recv=%d (stale FIFO?)\n",
+                   asc_cpu_running(sep_asc), asc_can_recv(sep_asc));
+
+        bool sent = asc_send(sep_asc, &msg_getrand);
+        if (dbg)
+            printf("SEP: [dbg] getrand asc_send = %d\n", sent);
+        if (!sent) {
+            sep_dbg_done = true;
             return done;
-        if (!asc_recv_timeout(sep_asc, &reply, SEP_TIMEOUT))
+        }
+
+        bool got = asc_recv_timeout(sep_asc, &reply, SEP_TIMEOUT);
+        if (dbg) {
+            printf("SEP: [dbg] getrand recv(1ms) = %d msg0=%016lx\n", got,
+                   got ? reply.msg0 : 0UL);
+            if (!got) {
+                /* Give a slow/slept SEP a long window before calling it dead. */
+                got = asc_recv_timeout(sep_asc, &reply, SEP_TIMEOUT_DBG);
+                printf("SEP: [dbg] getrand recv(200ms retry) = %d msg0=%016lx\n",
+                       got, got ? reply.msg0 : 0UL);
+            }
+            sep_dbg_done = true;
+        }
+        if (!got)
             return done;
         if (FIELD_GET(SEP_MSG_CMD, reply.msg0) != SEP_REPLY_GETRAND) {
             printf("SEP: unexpected getrand reply: %016lx\n", reply.msg0);
